1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:01,057
How does a model learn?

2
00:00:01,322 --> 00:00:02,117
It rolls downhill.

3
00:00:02,382 --> 00:00:03,355
Start with one weight.

4
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,522
Zoom into any point, and the curve turns

5
00:00:06,522 --> 00:00:07,148
into a line.

6
00:00:07,461 --> 00:00:08,817
Its slope is the gradient.

7
00:00:12,862 --> 00:00:14,002
Step against the slope.

8
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:16,382
The new weight is the old weight, minus

9
00:00:16,382 --> 00:00:18,067
the learning rate times the slope.

10
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:20,397
The flatter the curve, the smaller the

11
00:00:20,397 --> 00:00:20,645
step.

12
00:00:22,244 --> 00:00:23,465
Now, change the learning rate.

13
00:00:24,284 --> 00:00:25,348
Too small, and it crawls.

14
00:00:26,904 --> 00:00:28,006
Just right, and it settles.

15
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:30,245
Too big, and it overshoots.

16
00:00:32,324 --> 00:00:34,412
Add a second weight, and the curve becomes

17
00:00:34,412 --> 00:00:34,876
a surface.

18
00:00:35,374 --> 00:00:37,103
Now there are two slopes, one per weight.

19
00:00:38,014 --> 00:00:39,442
Together, they are the gradient.

20
00:00:39,954 --> 00:00:42,080
Step against it: the same rule works in

21
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:42,741
any dimension.

22
00:00:46,546 --> 00:00:47,399
Billions of weights.

23
00:00:47,655 --> 00:00:48,040
One rule.

